        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Red Cliffs</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">Winter of 208–209 CE; historians differ on the timeline of events</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> in Jing Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; the exact site is disputed, with candidates southwest of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> and near Chibi City in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> (commander Zhou Yu), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> and Liu Qi, about 50,000 marines in all, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, who claimed 800,000 men while Zhou Yu estimated about 230,000</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The allies drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>'s fleet and army back; Cao Cao escaped north and never again commanded so large a fleet, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> kept the lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</span></div>
