    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>'s southern campaign, begun in the autumn of 208, brought him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangling">Jiangling</a> and a large fleet before it was halted on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</li><li>The allies fought on the river, and a feigned surrender by Huang Gai set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>'s chained ships ablaze.</li><li>The battle fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> as a frontier, the basis of the later states of Shu Han and Eastern Wu, though its exact site is still disputed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> claiming an army of 800,000, while Zhou Yu put his strength nearer 230,000, including between 70 and 80 thousand men impressed from Ying.</p></aside>
    </section>

